WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.330
Forty-five pupils and eight phones,

2
00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.410
borrowed from parents for the afternoon.

3
00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:07.970
If one pupil holds each phone,

4
00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.610
thirty-seven of them sit and watch.

5
00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.470
So every pupil gets a job.

6
00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:17.930
Today: pair programming, group roles and

7
00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:20.750
live coding, for classes with few devices.

8
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.220
In pair programming, two

9
00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.940
pupils share one device.

10
00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:29.050
The driver types or taps.

11
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.700
The navigator reads, checks every line

12
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.660
and thinks about what comes next.

13
00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:37.530
They swap roles every five to ten

14
00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.250
minutes, so both do both.

15
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.160
With four pupils to a phone,

16
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.620
add two more roles.

17
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.150
The tester predicts what the

18
00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:48.450
program will do and checks it.

19
00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.510
The reporter writes down what worked and

20
00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.790
what didn’t, and shares it with the class.

21
00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.400
Rotate the roles each task.

22
00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.300
Pairs aren’t just a way to share devices.

23
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.700
Studies in universities found that pairs

24
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.300
produced better programs and more

25
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.740
students stayed with programming.

26
00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.330
Talking about code out loud

27
00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:11.950
catches bugs early.

28
00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.310
And many beginners say they

29
00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:15.430
feel more confident.

30
00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.620
With one phone, code live from the front.

31
00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.290
Hold the phone up or copy the code

32
00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:23.470
onto the board as you type.

33
00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.130
Stop before each run and

34
00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.850
ask: what will happen?

35
00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:30.910
Make a mistake on purpose, and

36
00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:32.310
let the class find it.

37
00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:33.950
Quick check!

38
00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:37.550
A pair has worked for twenty minutes, and

39
00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:39.690
the same pupil has typed the whole time.

40
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:41.140
What should happen?

41
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:42.880
Swap!

42
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.320
Set a timer, or swap at each task.

43
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.300
Both pupils must do both jobs.

44
00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.260
And watch that the navigator really

45
00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.780
navigates: reading, checking,

46
00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.220
suggesting, not just watching.

47
00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.430
Prepare phones the day before,

48
00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:01.770
where there’s a connection.

49
00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.770
Download the lessons you’ll use, and

50
00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.610
open one code step once, so Python

51
00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.230
is saved for offline use.

52
00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.900
In class, everything then

53
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.260
runs with no internet.

54
00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:17.130
Debugging is a great job for pairs.

55
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.360
The navigator reads the

56
00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:20.320
error message aloud.

57
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.420
The driver points to the line.

58
00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.070
Together they agree on one

59
00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:26.890
change before running again.

60
00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:30.130
In the app, open Bug hunt

61
00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:31.330
and play it in pairs.

62
00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.040
The navigator reads each error

63
00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.200
aloud; the driver fixes it.

64
00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.580
Swap after every bug, and talk about bug

65
00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:42.120
three, the one with no error message.

66
00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:44.170
Your turn.

67
00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.970
Tomorrow’s class has no internet.

68
00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.450
What must you do today?

69
00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:51.400
Download today.

70
00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.910
Remember: in pairs, a driver and a

71
00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:56.850
navigator swap every few minutes.

72
00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:00.660
In fours, add a tester and a reporter.

73
00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:03.460
Code live from the front, and

74
00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:04.420
let the class predict.

75
00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.310
And prepare phones the day before.

76
00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.430
Try it in class tomorrow: one programming

77
00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:14.250
task in pairs or fours, with role cards.

78
00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:16.960
Set a timer to swap roles

79
00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:18.020
every seven minutes.

80
00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:21.390
At the end, ask each reporter for one

81
00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.890
thing that worked and one bug they found.
